  "query_preview": "..A weakening shortwave trough is currently swinging \nacross northern Nevada and will cross far northern Utah tonight.\nMuch like last night, this fast-moving wave will push a \nreinforcing cold front into northern Utah, bringing valley rain \nshowers and light mountain snow once again. Precipitation is \nalready ongoing near remnants of the existing boundary and will \ncontinue through late tonight. The boundary and associated \nprecipitation is expected to remain north of around Utah County. \nExpect precipitation to wind down well before sunrise.\n\nFairly benign weather is expected on Wednesday and Thursday under\na southwesterly flow aloft. Temperatures should cool slightly \nfrom today across northern Utah owing the the cold front, then\nrebound for Thursday. Models do depict yet another weakening \ntrailing shortwave disturbance crossing Utah late Wednesday \nafternoon into Wednesday evening. The dynamics associated with \nthat looks to be very insignificant, so very little if any \nprecipitation is expected.\n\nMain focus continues to be the storm system for the end of the\nweek through the weekend. A low pressure system will slowly make\nits way to the PacNW coast on Thursday. On Friday, a subtle\ndisturbance rounding the base of this low and lifting across Utah\nwill start to spread some moisture into the area. However, with\nweak dynamics, expect mainly isolated to scattered showers and a\nfew thunderstorms to develop. Main focus for heavier \nprecipitation will be southwest Utah, otherwise amounts are \nexpected to remain relatively light elsewhere. The 25th percentile\nof the NBM has no measurable precipitation for the vast majority \nof the forecast area and only a few hundredths of an inch in \nportions of southwest Utah. However, the 75th percentile has \nbetween 0.5-1 inch over portions of southwest Utah, so cannot rule\nout some heavier showers and isolated potential for flash \nflooding. On Saturday, the storm system will begin to move inland.\nThis will maintain unsettled weather over the area, but with the \nbetter large-scale dynamics still upstream, expect generally \nisolated to scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms once \nagain. \n\nThe storm system is expected to cross Utah on Sunday, although \nthere is still some uncertainty regarding how deep it will carve \ninto the area. This storm will also phase with a southern stream \nsystem that will be associated with an atmospheric river. The \nmoisture plume will punch inland across far southern California \nand northern Baja. Some of this moisture will make it into Utah, \nwith IVT anomalies in the 97th-99th percentile of climatology. \nHowever, there is still enough model spread regarding how much \nprecipitation will develop over Utah. While the spread has \ndecreased from what models were showing 24 hours ago, we are still\nlooking at NBM 25th-75th percentile differences of 1-2 inches in \nareas favored in southwest flow and 0.5-1 inch elsewhere. \nRegardless, Sunday is expected to see the heaviest precipitation \nof this storm cycle, with the potential for widespread and \nmoderate to heavy precipitation with an increased flash flood \nthreat.\n\nA cold front will also push into Utah on Sunday, bringing lowering\nsnow levels. Snow amounts will be dependent on the strength of the\ntrough and precipitation amounts, of which, as mentioned, there \nis still significant uncertainty. However, a general 3-6 inches\nwith locally 10 inches or more in higher elevation sites such as \nnear Brian Head, in the Tushars, the upper Cottonwoods, and high \nUintas seem reasonable at this time.\n\nPrecipitation looks to mostly wind down Monday morning, leaving a\ncool northerly flow in place over the area. Models then start to\ndiverge on the large-scale pattern, with how quickly the storm\ncontinues to progress east out of the area and whether a strong\nridge builds into the area behind it or whether another storm \nsystem manages to move into the area midweek.",
